<p>Hello Negative360! let's take a look :)</p>
<p><strong>Some stuff:</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch:</p>
<p>It's a monastery( or Temple) that enlightens you.<br />
This temple answers only the question that your heart yearns for.<br />
Their is this room called &quot;The room of enlightenment&quot; where a mysterious individual will answer your question.<br />
Their are monks their that take care of the Temple, and the Foundation and the Monks reached a agreement in order study this SCP.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall, I don't mind the core anomaly, my main concern comes from the from the fact that there isn't much done with the idea. There isn't much that really grabs my attention or gets me invested in the characters, the 'enlightenment' element isn't really explored or given much detail. Overall I'd say the core anomaly is a little bare bones, as there isn't much other than the base idea.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: The story content will mostly consist of how the foundation came into custody of this SCP, and the agreement between the foundation and the Monks. (Edit: This will be part of the Article but I have another idea)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is quite flat, there isn't much of a set-up, middle act and payoff, the story overall seems to lack much of an arc, which makes it hard to become invested in the skip. The lack of clear characters also contributes to this, as I as the reader don't have much to really relate to. Overall, I'm left unsure on what is being tested, what is learnt by the end, what this agreement entails (and how it was reached), I'm also left unsure how exactly the Foundation found the skip?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Central Narrative will be following an man who has a lot questions, struggling through life, struggling to find meaning. Until he met this SCP who gave him the exact advice he needed.( I know this is kind of vague, but I am still trying to write out all the kinks.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is rather vague, as it doesn't answer or contribute to answering any of the questions I got while reading, what ends up happening to him? why will that be important?</p>
<p><strong>Hook:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hook/Attention-Grabber: Since this SCP can answer questions, it is possible that the foundation would try to use to learn more about other SCP's.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I wouldn't recommend you use &quot;it could teach us about other SCPs&quot; as the focus, the skip itself needs to be able to stand on its own without relying on questions and answers that are raised from other articles. I would recommend you put the focus more on the skip itself, as it would allow you to develop the idea (and the characters for that matter) further, without the article becoming reliant on other skips.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no Greenlight.</p>
<p>If you would like me to give this (or any concept) further advice/feedback, then feel welcome to shoot me a PM or ask via IRC. response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques in general, then I couldn't recommend the Butterfly squad more:)</p>
</div>
</div>
